Rem ==================================================================
Rem csv.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: CSV, RFC 4180
Rem
Rem A complete reader and writer, pure Shoddy. A document is the plain
Rem thing it looks like — a List Of List Of String, rows of cells — so
Rem seq.shoddy's Map, Filter and Fold work on it with nothing learned.
Rem
Rem     Let rows = CsvLoad("grades.csv")
Rem     Each(rows, Fn(r) => Print(Join(r, " | ")))
Rem     CsvSave("out.csv", Map(rows, Reverse))
Rem
Rem THREE LAYERS, each one word away from the next. Take the one that
Rem fits the file in front of you.
Rem
Rem   1. ROWS. CsvParse / CsvLoad in, CsvText / CsvSave out. Cells are
Rem      strings; what they mean is your business.
Rem
Rem   2. A SHEET. CsvSheetRead treats the first record as a header and
Rem      gives you a CsvSheet, after which columns have NAMES:
Rem
Rem          Let sh = CsvSheetLoad("people.csv")
Rem          Each(CsvBody(sh), Fn(r) => Print(CsvGet(sh, r, "NAME")))
Rem          Print(Str(Sum(CsvColumnNums(sh, "SCORE"))))
Rem
Rem      A row can also be handed over as an association list —
Rem      CsvPairs — which is precisely dict.shoddy's dictionary, so
Rem      DictGet and friends work on it unchanged, exactly as json's
Rem      objects do.
Rem
Rem   3. A TYPE. Give CsvRecs a function from cells to your record and
Rem      it hands back a List Of your record; give CsvFromRecs the
Rem      inverse and it hands back the file. The type is yours, this
Rem      machine never sees it:
Rem
Rem          Type Student
Rem              Name  As String
Rem              Score As Number
Rem
Rem          Def StudentOf(cells As List Of String) As Student
Rem              Student(Nth(cells, 1), Val(Nth(cells, 2)))
Rem
Rem          Def StudentCells(s As Student) As List Of String
Rem              { Name(s), Str(Score(s)) }
Rem
Rem          Let class = CsvLoadRecs("class.csv", StudentOf)
Rem          CsvSaveRecs("class.csv", class, StudentCells)
Rem
Rem      With a header, name the columns instead of counting them:
Rem
Rem          Let sh = CsvSheetLoad("class.csv")
Rem          Let class = CsvSheetRecs(sh, Fn(r) =>
Rem              Student(CsvGet(sh, r, "NAME"), CsvNum(sh, r, "SCORE")))
Rem
Rem The guard-pair convention, as in json.shoddy and str.shoddy: the
Rem strict word is the one you reach for first, and the total word is
Rem there the moment the input stops being yours.
Rem
Rem     CsvParse / CsvRead        CsvGet / CsvGetOr    CsvNum / CsvNumOr
Rem
Rem Shoddy has no catchable errors, so a parser that could only abort
Rem would be untestable on malformed input and unusable on untrusted
Rem input. CsvRead is therefore total: it returns CsvOk or CsvErr, and
Rem CsvErr carries the reason and the 1-based offset where the parse
Rem gave up. CsvParse is the aborting wrapper over it.
Rem
Rem ---- dialects ------------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem Every reader and writer word comes in two spellings — CsvParse and
Rem CsvParseAs, CsvText and CsvTextAs — the plain one being the -As one
Rem applied to CsvComma(). A CsvDialect carries the separator, the quote
Rem character, the line ending written out, whether spaces around an
Rem unquoted cell are dropped, and a comment prefix:
Rem
Rem     CsvComma()   ,  quote  LF     the default
Rem     CsvRfc()     ,  quote  CRLF   RFC 4180 to the letter
Rem     CsvTabs()    tab             TSV
Rem     CsvSemis()   ;               the European spreadsheet spelling
Rem     CsvSepBy(c)  any single character
Rem
Rem and two combinators, which take a dialect and return a new one:
Rem
Rem     CsvTrimming(d)      drop spaces around UNQUOTED cells
Rem     CsvCommented(d, p)  skip whole records that begin with p
Rem
Rem So the repo's own numeric .dat files — comma separated, # comments —
Rem read with CsvParseAs(text, CsvCommented(CsvTrimming(CsvComma()), "#")).
Rem
Rem ---- what is quoted, and what is not -------------------------------
Rem
Rem Reading, a cell is quoted or it is not, and inside quotes a doubled
Rem quote ("") is one literal quote — that is the whole of RFC 4180's
Rem escaping and there is no backslash anywhere in it. A quoted cell may
Rem contain separators and line breaks. A bare quote in the middle of an
Rem UNQUOTED cell is taken literally (ab"c is four characters), which is
Rem what every spreadsheet does; a quote that opens a cell must close it.
Rem
Rem Writing, a cell is quoted only when it has to be: it holds the
Rem separator, a quote, a CR or an LF, or it has a leading or trailing
Rem space. That last one is not RFC 4180's rule — it is here so a round
Rem trip is exact under CsvTrimming as well, since otherwise the trimmer
Rem would eat spaces the writer meant.
Rem
Rem ---- known limits --------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem Line endings. LF, CRLF and lone CR all end a record on the way in,
Rem including inside a quoted cell, where they are normalized to LF so
Rem that a parse-write-parse round trip is stable. Going out, the
Rem dialect's ending is written after EVERY record, the last included —
Rem the same convention as file.shoddy's WriteLines.
Rem
Rem A blank line is a record of one empty cell, as RFC 4180 says it is.
Rem A single line ending at the very end of the input is the terminator
Rem of the last record and does NOT start an empty one. If you want
Rem blank lines gone, CsvDropBlanks is one word.
Rem
Rem Ragged rows are not an error. Files in the wild are ragged, and a
Rem reader that aborted on them would be useless for the job you have.
Rem CsvIsRect asks, CsvWidth measures, CsvRect pads to the widest row.
Rem
Rem A byte-order mark is stripped, both the UTF-8 spelling (three chars,
Rem since file i/o is one byte per char) and a bare U+FEFF.
Rem
Rem NUL cannot be carried. Chr(0) is the empty string in Shoddy, so a
Rem NUL in the file would silently shorten a cell — but nothing here can
Rem produce one, and a CSV file containing one is not text.
Rem
Rem Numbers are Str and Val, so a cell written from a Number carries ten
Rem significant digits (see json.shoddy on the same point). Values that
Rem survive ten digits round-trip exactly.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "dict.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"

Rem The document a total read produces.
Type CsvDoc = CsvOk(CsvRows As List Of List Of String) | CsvErr(CsvWhy As String, CsvAt As Number)

Rem The separator, the quote character, the line ending the writer puts
Rem between records, whether an unquoted cell is trimmed, and a comment
Rem prefix ("" for none). Build one with CsvComma and friends below
Rem rather than by hand — record fields are positional, so a dialect
Rem written out longhand is a thing that breaks quietly if a field is
Rem ever inserted.
Type CsvDialect
    CsvSep   As String
    CsvQuote As String
    CsvEol   As String
    CsvTrims As Boolean
    CsvSkip  As String

Rem A document read as a header and the records under it.
Type CsvSheet
    CsvHeads As List Of String
    CsvBody  As List Of List Of String

Rem The internal cell result, threaded through the scanner: the cell's
Rem text AND the 1-based index of the first character after it, or why
Rem and where the scan gave up.
Type CTake = CTook(CTVal As String, CTNext As Number) | CTBad(CTWhy As String, CTAt As Number)

Rem The same, one level up, for a whole record.
Type CRTake = CRTook(CRVal As List Of String, CRNext As Number) | CRBad(CRWhy As String, CRAt As Number)

Rem ---- constants -------------------------------------------------------
Rem Machines cannot carry a top-level Let, so the constants are zero-arg
Rem Defs, as json's JQt() and vt100's Esc() are.

Def CsvQt() As String
    Chr(34)

Def CsvNl() As String
    Chr(10)

Def CsvCr() As String
    Chr(13)

Def CsvTab() As String
    Chr(9)

Rem ---- dialects --------------------------------------------------------

Def CsvComma() As CsvDialect
    CsvDialect(",", CsvQt(), CsvNl(), False, "")

Rem RFC 4180 says CRLF between records. Everything else in this library
Rem writes LF, so that is the default and this is the opt-in.
Def CsvRfc() As CsvDialect
    CsvDialect(",", CsvQt(), CsvCr() & CsvNl(), False, "")

Def CsvTabs() As CsvDialect
    CsvDialect(CsvTab(), CsvQt(), CsvNl(), False, "")

Def CsvSemis() As CsvDialect
    CsvDialect(";", CsvQt(), CsvNl(), False, "")

Rem A separator is one character, and not one the grammar already spends
Rem — that is a programmer's mistake rather than a reader's, so it
Rem aborts here instead of turning into a strange parse later.
Def CsvSepBy(c As String) As CsvDialect
    If Len(c) <> 1 Then
        Error("CSVSEPBY: A SEPARATOR IS EXACTLY ONE CHARACTER")
    Else
        If c = CsvQt() Or c = CsvCr() Or c = CsvNl() Then
            Error("CSVSEPBY: A QUOTE, CR OR LF CANNOT BE THE SEPARATOR")
        Else
            CsvDialect(c, CsvQt(), CsvNl(), False, "")

Rem Spaces around an UNQUOTED cell are dropped. Quoted cells are left
Rem exactly as written — quoting is how a file says "these spaces are
Rem mine".
Def CsvTrimming(d As CsvDialect) As CsvDialect
    CsvDialect(CsvSep(d), CsvQuote(d), CsvEol(d), True, CsvSkip(d))

Rem Records beginning with the prefix are skipped whole. Not part of
Rem CSV; part of every hand-kept data file there has ever been.
Def CsvCommented(d As CsvDialect, prefix As String) As CsvDialect
    CsvDialect(CsvSep(d), CsvQuote(d), CsvEol(d), CsvTrims(d), prefix)

Rem ======================================================================
Rem THE READER
Rem ======================================================================

Rem The slice [run, i), or "" — Mid is never asked for zero characters.
Def CSlice(s As String, run As Number, i As Number) As String
    If i > run Then
        Mid(s, run, i - run)
    Else
        ""

Rem Flush the run [run, i) onto the fragment list, if it is not empty.
Def CRun(s As String, run As Number, i As Number, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    If i > run Then
        Prepend(Mid(s, run, i - run), acc)
    Else
        acc

Rem True where a cell ends: the separator, either line ending character,
Rem or the end of the input.
Def CEnds(c As String, d As CsvDialect) As Boolean
    Len(c) = 0 Or c = CsvSep(d) Or c = CsvNl() Or c = CsvCr()

Rem The index just past the end-of-record at i, or 0 if there is not one
Rem there. CRLF counts once.
Def CEol(s As String, i As Number) As Number
    Let c = Mid(s, i, 1)
    If c = CsvCr() Then
        Ifte(Mid(s, i + 1, 1) = CsvNl(), i + 2, i + 1)
    Else
        Ifte(c = CsvNl(), i + 1, 0)

Rem The index just past the line containing i — for comment records,
Rem which are not parsed at all. Self-tail-recursive, so a long comment
Rem costs no stack.
Def CToEol(s As String, i As Number) As Number
    If i > Len(s) Then
        i
    Else
        If CEol(s, i) > 0 Then
            CEol(s, i)
        Else
            CToEol(s, i + 1)

Def CComment(s As String, i As Number, d As CsvDialect) As Boolean
    Len(CsvSkip(d)) > 0 And Mid(s, i, Len(CsvSkip(d))) = CsvSkip(d)

Rem ---- cells -----------------------------------------------------------

Def CTrimmed(t As String, d As CsvDialect) As String
    If CsvTrims(d) Then
        Trim(t)
    Else
        t

Rem A CR or CRLF inside a quoted cell becomes an LF, so that reading a
Rem file written on the other kind of machine and writing it back gives
Rem the same document rather than a longer one.
Def CNorm(t As String) As String
    Replace(Replace(t, CsvCr() & CsvNl(), CsvNl()), CsvCr(), CsvNl())

Rem An unquoted cell: everything up to the next separator or line
Rem ending. A quote in here is an ordinary character.
Def CBare(s As String, i As Number, run As Number, d As CsvDialect) As CTake
    If CEnds(Mid(s, i, 1), d) Then
        CTook(CTrimmed(CSlice(s, run, i), d), i)
    Else
        CBare(s, i + 1, run, d)

Rem A quoted cell, from the character after its opening quote. As in
Rem json's string scanner, the start of the current unescaped RUN is
Rem tracked so an ordinary cell costs one fragment rather than one per
Rem character. A doubled quote is one literal quote; any other quote
Rem closes the cell.
Def CQuoted(s As String, i As Number, run As Number, acc As List Of String, d As CsvDialect) As CTake
    If i > Len(s) Then
        CTBad("UNTERMINATED QUOTED CELL", i)
    Else
        Let c = Mid(s, i, 1)
        If c <> CsvQuote(d) Then
            CQuoted(s, i + 1, run, acc, d)
        Else
            If Mid(s, i + 1, 1) = CsvQuote(d) Then
                CQuoted(s, i + 2, i + 2, Prepend(CsvQuote(d), CRun(s, run, i, acc)), d)
            Else
                CClose(s, i + 1, Join(Reverse(CRun(s, run, i, acc)), ""), d)

Rem After the closing quote nothing may follow but a separator or the
Rem end of the record. "AB"C is the one shape this reader refuses, and
Rem it refuses it because there is no reading of it that is not a guess.
Def CClose(s As String, i As Number, body As String, d As CsvDialect) As CTake
    If CEnds(Mid(s, i, 1), d) Then
        CTook(CNorm(body), i)
    Else
        CTBad("EXPECTED A SEPARATOR OR AN END OF RECORD AFTER A CLOSING QUOTE", i)

Def CCell(s As String, i As Number, d As CsvDialect) As CTake
    If Mid(s, i, 1) = CsvQuote(d) Then
        CQuoted(s, i + 1, i + 1, { }, d)
    Else
        CBare(s, i, i, d)

Rem ---- records ---------------------------------------------------------
Rem One Def, not two, so the cell loop is a SELF tail call and a wide
Rem record costs no stack.

Def CRecord(s As String, i As Number, acc As List Of String, d As CsvDialect) As CRTake
    Select Case CCell(s, i, d)
        Case CTBad(why, at)
            CRBad(why, at)
        Case CTook(v, n)
            If Mid(s, n, 1) = CsvSep(d) Then
                CRecord(s, n + 1, Prepend(v, acc), d)
            Else
                CRTook(Reverse(Prepend(v, acc)), n)
        Case Else
            CRBad("UNEXPECTED END OF INPUT", i)

Rem The record loop. Also a self tail call, so a file of a hundred
Rem thousand rows costs no more stack than a file of one — the reader
Rem has no recursion in it that grows with the input.
Def CRecords(s As String, i As Number, acc As List Of List Of String, d As CsvDialect) As CsvDoc
    If i > Len(s) Then
        CsvOk(Reverse(acc))
    Else
        If CComment(s, i, d) Then
            CRecords(s, CToEol(s, i), acc, d)
        Else
            Select Case CRecord(s, i, { }, d)
                Case CRBad(why, at)
                    CsvErr(why, at)
                Case CRTook(row, n)
                    Let k = CEol(s, n)
                    If n > Len(s) Then
                        CsvOk(Reverse(Prepend(row, acc)))
                    Else
                        If k = 0 Then
                            CsvErr("EXPECTED A SEPARATOR OR AN END OF RECORD", n)
                        Else
                            CRecords(s, k, Prepend(row, acc), d)
                Case Else
                    CsvErr("UNEXPECTED END OF INPUT", i)

Rem ---- the public reader -----------------------------------------------

Rem Excel writes one; nothing downstream wants it in the first header.
Def CsvNoBom(s As String) As String
    If StartsWith(s, Chr(239) & Chr(187) & Chr(191)) Then
        Mid(s, 4, Len(s))
    Else
        If StartsWith(s, Chr(65279)) Then
            Mid(s, 2, Len(s))
        Else
            s

Def CsvReadAs(s As String, d As CsvDialect) As CsvDoc
    CRecords(CsvNoBom(s), 1, { }, d)

Def CsvRead(s As String) As CsvDoc
    CsvReadAs(s, CsvComma())

Def CsvParseAs(s As String, d As CsvDialect) As List Of List Of String
    Select Case CsvReadAs(s, d)
        Case CsvOk(rows)
            rows
        Case CsvErr(why, at)
            Error("CSVPARSE: " & why & " AT " & Str(at))
        Case Else
            Error("CSVPARSE: UNREACHABLE")

Def CsvParse(s As String) As List Of List Of String
    CsvParseAs(s, CsvComma())

Def CsvLoadAs(path As String, d As CsvDialect) As List Of List Of String
    CsvParseAs(ReadFile(path), d)

Def CsvLoad(path As String) As List Of List Of String
    CsvParse(ReadFile(path))

Rem ======================================================================
Rem THE WRITER
Rem ======================================================================
Rem Never build a string with & in a left fold — that is quadratic in the
Rem output, which is exactly the shape of a serializer. Every writer word
Rem prepends FRAGMENTS onto an accumulator and the join happens once, at
Rem the top, in CsvTextAs.

Rem One line and not three: a wrapped Or outside brackets takes its left
Rem operand off the stack rather than from the line above it.
Def CsvNeedsQuote(t As String, d As CsvDialect) As Boolean
    Instr(t, CsvSep(d)) > 0 Or Instr(t, CsvQuote(d)) > 0 Or Instr(t, CsvNl()) > 0 Or Instr(t, CsvCr()) > 0 Or t <> Trim(t)

Rem Wrapped in quotes with every inner quote doubled. RFC 4180's escape
Rem is the whole of RFC 4180's escaping.
Def CsvQuoteCell(t As String, d As CsvDialect) As String
    CsvQuote(d) & Replace(t, CsvQuote(d), CsvQuote(d) & CsvQuote(d)) & CsvQuote(d)

Def CsvCellAs(t As String, d As CsvDialect) As String
    If CsvNeedsQuote(t, d) Then
        CsvQuoteCell(t, d)
    Else
        t

Def CsvCellOf(t As String) As String
    CsvCellAs(t, CsvComma())

Rem One record, without its line ending — for appending to an open file,
Rem or printing.
Def CsvLineAs(cells As List Of String, d As CsvDialect) As String
    Join(Reverse(CFragCells(cells, d, { }, True)), "")

Def CsvLine(cells As List Of String) As String
    CsvLineAs(cells, CsvComma())

Rem The flag is named lead, not first: words are case-folded and a local
Rem outranks a builtin, so a parameter named first would shadow First
Rem everywhere in the Def.
Def CFragCells(cells As List Of String, d As CsvDialect, acc As List Of String, lead As Boolean) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(cells) Then
        acc
    Else
        If lead Then
            CFragCells(Rest(cells), d, Prepend(CsvCellAs(First(cells), d), acc), False)
        Else
            CFragCells(Rest(cells), d, Prepend(CsvCellAs(First(cells), d), Prepend(CsvSep(d), acc)), False)

Def CFragRows(rows As List Of List Of String, d As CsvDialect, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(rows) Then
        acc
    Else
        CFragRows(Rest(rows), d, Prepend(CsvEol(d), CFragCells(First(rows), d, acc, True)))

Def CsvTextAs(rows As List Of List Of String, d As CsvDialect) As String
    Join(Reverse(CFragRows(rows, d, { })), "")

Def CsvText(rows As List Of List Of String) As String
    CsvTextAs(rows, CsvComma())

Def CsvSaveAs(path As String, rows As List Of List Of String, d As CsvDialect)
    WriteFile(path, CsvTextAs(rows, d))

Def CsvSave(path As String, rows As List Of List Of String)
    WriteFile(path, CsvText(rows))

Def CsvAppendAs(path As String, rows As List Of List Of String, d As CsvDialect)
    AppendFile(path, CsvTextAs(rows, d))

Def CsvAppend(path As String, rows As List Of List Of String)
    AppendFile(path, CsvText(rows))

Rem ======================================================================
Rem SHAPE
Rem ======================================================================
Rem Ragged is normal. These are the words for finding out, and for
Rem deciding what to do about it.

Def CsvWidth(rows As List Of List Of String) As Number
    Fold(rows, 0, Fn(a, r) => Max(a, Length(r)))

Rem The width is measured ONCE and carried in. Fn(r) => Length(r) =
Rem CsvWidth(rows) would re-measure the whole document for every row it
Rem checked, which is a quadratic scan hiding inside a one-line word.
Def CsvIsRect(rows As List Of List Of String) As Boolean
    Let w = CsvWidth(rows)
    All(rows, Fn(r) => Length(r) = w)

Def CsvPadRow(row As List Of String, n As Number) As List Of String
    If Length(row) >= n Then
        row
    Else
        CsvPadRow(Append(row, ""), n)

Rem Every row padded with empty cells to the width of the widest.
Def CsvRect(rows As List Of List Of String) As List Of List Of String
    Let w = CsvWidth(rows)
    Map(rows, Fn(r) => CsvPadRow(r, w))

Rem A blank line reads as a record of one empty cell. This drops those,
Rem and any other row with nothing in it.
Def CsvDropBlanks(rows As List Of List Of String) As List Of List Of String
    Filter(rows, Fn(r) => Not All(r, Fn(t) => Len(Trim(t)) = 0))

Rem A List is a quotation and compares by identity, so = on two rows is
Rem always False however identical they are. This is the comparison you
Rem want after a round trip — the same office JEqual does in json.
Def CsvRowEqual(a As List Of String, b As List Of String) As Boolean
    Length(a) = Length(b) And All(Zip(a, b), Fn(p) => Fst(p) = Snd(p))

Def CsvEqual(a As List Of List Of String, b As List Of List Of String) As Boolean
    Length(a) = Length(b) And All(Zip(a, b), Fn(p) => CsvRowEqual(Fst(p), Snd(p)))

Rem The transpose — columns become rows. Useful for a file that is
Rem written the other way round, and for column-wise arithmetic.
Def CsvFlip(rows As List Of List Of String) As List Of List Of String
    Let w = CsvWidth(rows)
    Let sq = CsvRect(rows)
    Map(Range(1, w), Fn(k) => Map(sq, Fn(r) => Nth(r, k)))

Rem ======================================================================
Rem SHEETS — A HEADER AND THE RECORDS UNDER IT
Rem ======================================================================

Def CsvSheetOf(rows As List Of List Of String) As CsvSheet
    If IsEmpty(rows) Then
        CsvSheet({ }, { })
    Else
        CsvSheet(First(rows), Rest(rows))

Def CsvSheetReadAs(s As String, d As CsvDialect) As CsvSheet
    CsvSheetOf(CsvParseAs(s, d))

Def CsvSheetRead(s As String) As CsvSheet
    CsvSheetOf(CsvParse(s))

Def CsvSheetLoadAs(path As String, d As CsvDialect) As CsvSheet
    CsvSheetOf(CsvLoadAs(path, d))

Def CsvSheetLoad(path As String) As CsvSheet
    CsvSheetOf(CsvLoad(path))

Rem Back to rows, header first — the inverse of CsvSheetOf.
Def CsvSheetRows(sh As CsvSheet) As List Of List Of String
    Prepend(CsvHeads(sh), CsvBody(sh))

Def CsvSheetTextAs(sh As CsvSheet, d As CsvDialect) As String
    CsvTextAs(CsvSheetRows(sh), d)

Def CsvSheetText(sh As CsvSheet) As String
    CsvText(CsvSheetRows(sh))

Def CsvSheetSaveAs(path As String, sh As CsvSheet, d As CsvDialect)
    WriteFile(path, CsvSheetTextAs(sh, d))

Def CsvSheetSave(path As String, sh As CsvSheet)
    WriteFile(path, CsvSheetText(sh))

Rem ---- columns by name -------------------------------------------------
Rem The parameter is head, not name: a record field called Name is
Rem common enough that shadowing its accessor inside this Def would be a
Rem trap laid for somebody else's program.

Def CsvIndexOf(sh As CsvSheet, head As String) As Number
    IndexOf(CsvHeads(sh), head)

Def CsvHas(sh As CsvSheet, head As String) As Boolean
    CsvIndexOf(sh, head) > 0

Def CsvCount(sh As CsvSheet) As Number
    Length(CsvBody(sh))

Rem The strict half of the guard pair: no such column, or a row too
Rem short to reach it, is an error and not an empty string. A file whose
Rem header says SCORE and whose rows do not have one is a file you want
Rem to hear about.
Def CsvGet(sh As CsvSheet, row As List Of String, head As String) As String
    Let k = CsvIndexOf(sh, head)
    If k = 0 Then
        Error("CSVGET: NO COLUMN '" & head & "'")
    Else
        If k > Length(row) Then
            Error("CSVGET: ROW STOPS SHORT OF COLUMN '" & head & "'")
        Else
            Nth(row, k)

Def CsvGetOr(sh As CsvSheet, row As List Of String, head As String, dflt As String) As String
    Let k = CsvIndexOf(sh, head)
    If k = 0 Or k > Length(row) Then
        dflt
    Else
        Nth(row, k)

Def CsvNum(sh As CsvSheet, row As List Of String, head As String) As Number
    Val(Trim(CsvGet(sh, row, head)))

Def CsvNumOr(sh As CsvSheet, row As List Of String, head As String, dflt As Number) As Number
    ValOr(Trim(CsvGetOr(sh, row, head, "")), dflt)

Def CsvBool(sh As CsvSheet, row As List Of String, head As String) As Boolean
    ToBool(CsvGet(sh, row, head))

Def CsvBoolOr(sh As CsvSheet, row As List Of String, head As String, dflt As Boolean) As Boolean
    ToBoolOr(CsvGetOr(sh, row, head, ""), dflt)

Rem One column, top to bottom.
Def CsvColumn(sh As CsvSheet, head As String) As List Of String
    Map(CsvBody(sh), Fn(r) => CsvGet(sh, r, head))

Def CsvColumnNums(sh As CsvSheet, head As String) As List Of Number
    Map(CsvBody(sh), Fn(r) => CsvNum(sh, r, head))

Rem The rows for which a column holds v — the WHERE clause, near enough.
Def CsvWhere(sh As CsvSheet, head As String, v As String) As List Of List Of String
    Filter(CsvBody(sh), Fn(r) => CsvGetOr(sh, r, head, "") = v)

Rem ---- rows as dictionaries --------------------------------------------
Rem A header and a row zipped is an association list — key in Fst, value
Rem in Snd — which is precisely dict.shoddy's dictionary, so DictGet,
Rem DictHas and DictPut work on it unchanged, exactly as json's object
Rem members do. Zip stops at the shorter of the two, so a ragged row
Rem simply has fewer keys.

Def CsvPairs(sh As CsvSheet, row As List Of String) As List Of Pair
    Zip(CsvHeads(sh), row)

Def CsvDicts(sh As CsvSheet) As List Of List Of Pair
    Map(CsvBody(sh), Fn(r) => CsvPairs(sh, r))

Rem And back: a dictionary laid out under a header, missing keys empty.
Def CsvRowOfDict(heads As List Of String, d As List Of Pair) As List Of String
    Map(heads, Fn(h) => DictGetOr(d, h, ""))

Def CsvSheetOfDicts(heads As List Of String, ds As List Of List Of Pair) As CsvSheet
    CsvSheet(heads, Map(ds, Fn(d) => CsvRowOfDict(heads, d)))

Rem ======================================================================
Rem TYPES — A FILE OF RECORDS, AND RECORDS BACK TO A FILE
Rem ======================================================================
Rem This machine never learns your type. You pass the two functions that
Rem know it — cells to a record, and a record to cells — and everything
Rem here is Map over one of them. That is the whole trick, and it is why
Rem there is nothing to configure: a Type with four fields and a Type
Rem with forty go through the same four words.
Rem
Rem The parameter is rowOf, not of: Of is a keyword.

Def CsvRecsAs(s As String, d As CsvDialect, rowOf As [ List Of String -- t ]) As List Of t
    Map(CsvParseAs(s, d), rowOf)

Def CsvRecs(s As String, rowOf As [ List Of String -- t ]) As List Of t
    Map(CsvParse(s), rowOf)

Def CsvLoadRecsAs(path As String, d As CsvDialect, rowOf As [ List Of String -- t ]) As List Of t
    Map(CsvLoadAs(path, d), rowOf)

Def CsvLoadRecs(path As String, rowOf As [ List Of String -- t ]) As List Of t
    Map(CsvLoad(path), rowOf)

Rem The header is dropped and each remaining row becomes a record. Pass
Rem a closure over the sheet to name columns instead of counting them:
Rem
Rem     CsvSheetRecs(sh, Fn(r) => Student(CsvGet(sh, r, "NAME"),
Rem                                       CsvNum(sh, r, "SCORE")))
Def CsvSheetRecs(sh As CsvSheet, rowOf As [ List Of String -- t ]) As List Of t
    Map(CsvBody(sh), rowOf)

Def CsvFromRecsAs(xs As List Of t, d As CsvDialect, cellsOf As [ t -- List Of String ]) As String
    CsvTextAs(Map(xs, cellsOf), d)

Def CsvFromRecs(xs As List Of t, cellsOf As [ t -- List Of String ]) As String
    CsvText(Map(xs, cellsOf))

Def CsvSaveRecsAs(path As String, xs As List Of t, d As CsvDialect, cellsOf As [ t -- List Of String ])
    WriteFile(path, CsvFromRecsAs(xs, d, cellsOf))

Def CsvSaveRecs(path As String, xs As List Of t, cellsOf As [ t -- List Of String ])
    WriteFile(path, CsvFromRecs(xs, cellsOf))

Rem With a header line in front — the spelling you want for a file
Rem anything else is going to read.
Def CsvSheetOfRecs(heads As List Of String, xs As List Of t, cellsOf As [ t -- List Of String ]) As CsvSheet
    CsvSheet(heads, Map(xs, cellsOf))

Def CsvSaveSheetRecs(path As String, heads As List Of String, xs As List Of t, cellsOf As [ t -- List Of String ])
    CsvSheetSave(path, CsvSheetOfRecs(heads, xs, cellsOf))

Rem ======================================================================
Rem NUMBERS
Rem ======================================================================
Rem The column-picking numeric loader every mill was growing for itself:
Rem a file of numbers, comma separated, # for a comment, spaces ignored.

Def CsvNumeric() As CsvDialect
    CsvCommented(CsvTrimming(CsvComma()), "#")

Def CsvNums(rows As List Of List Of String) As List Of List Of Number
    Map(rows, Fn(r) => Map(r, Fn(t) => Val(Trim(t))))

Def CsvLoadNums(path As String) As List Of List Of Number
    CsvNums(CsvDropBlanks(CsvLoadAs(path, CsvNumeric())))

Def CsvFromNums(rows As List Of List Of Number) As String
    CsvText(Map(rows, Fn(r) => Map(r, Str)))

Def CsvSaveNums(path As String, rows As List Of List Of Number)
    WriteFile(path, CsvFromNums(rows))
